**A.** → Falsch

Work rates add und remaining work divided by the combined rate is the time still needed. $A$ does $\frac{1}{12}$ of the job per day, $B$ does $\frac{1}{18}$ und $C$ does $\frac{1}{24}$. Together $A$ und $B$ do $$
\frac{1}{12} + \frac{1}{18} = \frac{3}{36} + \frac{2}{36} = \frac{5}{36}
$$ of a job per day. In two days they finish $$
2 \cdot \frac{5}{36} = \frac{10}{36} = \frac{5}{18}
$$ so the remainder is $$
1 - \frac{5}{18} = \frac{13}{18}
$$ All three together do $$
\frac{1}{12} + \frac{1}{18} + \frac{1}{24} = \frac{6}{72} + \frac{4}{72} + \frac{3}{72} = \frac{13}{72}
$$ of a job per day. The remaining $\frac{13}{18}$ at that rate takes $$
\frac{13}{18} \cdot \frac{72}{13} = 4
$$ days, not $6$. The Behauptunged $6$ days at $\frac{13}{72}$ per day would cover $\frac{13}{12}$ of a job, more than the remainder.

Die Aussage ist falsch.

**C.** → Falsch

Four minutes of $A$ alone finish $\frac{4}{20} = \frac{1}{5}$ of the tank, so $\frac{4}{5}$ remains. Three minutes of $A$ und $B$ together finish $$
3\left(\frac{1}{20} + \frac{1}{30}\right) = 3 \cdot \frac{1}{12} = \frac{1}{4}
$$ so $\frac{4}{5} - \frac{1}{4} = \frac{11}{20}$ remains. With the leak as well the net rate is $$
\frac{1}{20} + \frac{1}{30} - \frac{1}{60} = \frac{1}{15}
$$ The remaining time is $$
t = \frac{11/20}{1/15} = \frac{33}{4}
$$ minutes, not $6$.

Die Aussage ist falsch.

**C.** → Falsch

Pipes $A$, $B$ und $C$ have rates $\frac{1}{6}$, $\frac{1}{8}$ und $\frac{1}{24}$ of the job per hour. Two hours of $A$ und $B$ finish $$
2\left(\frac{1}{6} + \frac{1}{8}\right) = 2 \cdot \frac{7}{24} = \frac{7}{12}
$$ so $\frac{5}{12}$ remains. All three together do $$
\frac{1}{6} + \frac{1}{8} + \frac{1}{24} = \frac{1}{3}
$$ of the job per hour, so the remaining time is $$
t = \frac{5/12}{1/3} = \frac{5}{4}
$$ hours, not $4$. Four more hours at $\frac{1}{3}$ per hour would cover $\frac{4}{3}$ of a job.

Die Aussage ist falsch.

MATH 4.37.solution_overview:
Es seien $r_A$ und $r_B$ denote the baseline production rates in reports per hour for Alex und Blair, respectively. From the given single-worker completion times, we have: $$r_A = \frac{1}{12}$$ $$r_B = \frac{1}{20}$$ When both analysts work together at their baseline rates, their combined hourly production rate $r_{\text{joint}}$ is the sum of their individual rates: $$
r_{\text{joint}} = r_A + r_B = \frac{1}{12} + \frac{1}{20}
$$ $$
r_{\text{joint}} = \frac{5}{60} + \frac{3}{60} = \frac{8}{60} = \frac{2}{15}
$$ Therefore, at baseline efficiency, the two analysts together complete $\frac{2}{15}$ of a report per hour.

MATH 4.40.solution_overview:
The total distance covered over the two legs is the sum of the distances traveled on each individual leg. Setting the total distance equal to $360$ km ergibt the linear model in the unknown $t$: $$v_1 t + v_2 (6 - t) = 360$$ Einsetzen von the expressions for $v_1$ und $v_2$: $$(k + 20)t + (2k + 10)(6 - t) = 360$$ Expanding the terms on the linke Seite: $$(k + 20)t + 6(2k + 10) - (2k + 10)t = 360$$ $$(k + 20 - 2k - 10)t + 12k + 60 = 360$$ $$(10 - k)t + 12k + 60 = 360$$ Isolating the linear term in $t$ on the linke Seite: $$(10 - k)t = 300 - 12k$$ Whenever $k \neq 10$, this lineare Gleichung has the unique algebraic solution: $$
t = \frac{300 - 12k}{10 - k} = \frac{12(25 - k)}{10 - k}
$$

MATH 4.46.solution_overview:
The conversion between the European score $E$ und the Global score $G$ is given by the linear model $G(E) = m \cdot E + b$. Using the two calibration points $(E_1, G_1) = (20, 50)$ und $(E_2, G_2) = (80, 140)$, we first determine the Steigung $m$: $$
m = \frac{G_2 - G_1}{E_2 - E_1} = \frac{140 - 50}{80 - 20} = \frac{90}{60} = \frac{3}{2}
$$ Next, we determine the intercept $b$ by substituting $(E_1, G_1) = (20, 50)$ into the lineare Gleichung: $$50 = \frac{3}{2} \cdot 20 + b$$ $$50 = 30 + b$$ $$b = 20$$ Thus, the explicit conversion function is given by: $$G(E) = \frac{3}{2} E + 20$$ Solving for $E$ in terms of $G$ ergibt the inverse conversion: $$
E(G) = \frac{2}{3}(G - 20) = \frac{2}{3}G - \frac{40}{3}
$$

MATH 4.50.solution_overview:
Wir beginnen damit, clearing the denominators in the given Gleichung for any admissible parameter $k \neq 1$. Multiplikation der gesamten Gleichung mit the gemeinsamer Nenner $2(k - 1)$ gives $$2(k x - 2) - (x + 3)(k - 1) = k - 7$$ Das Ausmultiplizieren beider Terme auf der linken Seite ergibt $$2k x - 4 - (k x - x + 3k - 3) = k - 7$$ $$2k x - 4 - k x + x - 3k + 3 = k - 7$$ $$(k + 1)x - 3k - 1 = k - 7$$ Das Isolieren des Terms mit $x$ führt auf die lineare Standardform $$(k + 1)x = 4k - 6$$ If $k = -1$, der Koeffizient von $x$ vanishes while the rechte Seite is $-10 \neq 0$, producing a contradiction. For all $k \in \mathbb{R} \setminus \{-1, 1\}$, die eindeutige Lösung is given by $$
x = \frac{4k - 6}{k + 1} = \frac{4(k + 1) - 10}{k + 1} = 4 - \frac{10}{k + 1}
$$

MATH 4.52.solution_overview:
The compensation model is a lineare Gleichung in the unknown $S$ with constant parameters $p$, $d$ und $K$: $$N = \left(1 + \frac{p}{100}\right)\left(1 - \frac{d}{100}\right)S - K$$ Expanding the composite multiplier of $S$ gives: $$
\left(1 + \frac{p}{100}\right)\left(1 - \frac{d}{100}\right) = \frac{(100 + p)(100 - d)}{10000} = 1 + \frac{p - d - \frac{pd}{100}}{100}
$$ Setting $N = S$ ergibt the standard form of a lineare Gleichung in one variable: $$\left[1 - \left(1 + \frac{p}{100}\right)\left(1 - \frac{d}{100}\right)\right]S = -K$$ The existence und uniqueness of solutions depend on whether der Koeffizient von $S$ vanishes und whether the constant term $-K$ is zero.

MATH 4.170.solution_overview:
An Gleichung that contains a logarithm only through the expression $t = \log_{b} x$ is reduced to an algebraic Gleichung in $t$. Each admissible value of $t$ is transferred back with the definition of the logarithm: $$
\log_{b} x = t \iff x = b^{t}
$$ Weil $b^{t} > 0$ for every real $t$, every reeller Wert of $t$ produces exactly one admissible $x$.

MATH 4.177.solution_overview:
Es seien $N(t)$ denote the size of the bacterial culture at time $t$, measured in hours, with initial size $N_0 = N(0)$. Weil the population triples every four hours, the growth follows an exponential model with base $3$: $$N(t) = N_0 \cdot 3^{\frac{t}{4}}$$ The hourly growth factor $a$ is the multiplicative factor per unit time of one hour, defined by $N(t+1) = a \cdot N(t)$: $$
a = 3^{\frac{1}{4}} = \sqrt[4]{3}
$$ For any elapsed time $t$, the fold-increase of the population relative to its initial state is given by the ratio: $$\frac{N(t)}{N_0} = 3^{\frac{t}{4}}$$
